Enhancement: Add optional SlateDB path history index

Vaultic can now maintain an optional `pv:` path history index for selected paths
or subtrees in SlateDB metadata. The index is keyed by filesystem id, path, and
commit sequence, so a path's binding changes can be scanned directly instead of
walking every retained snapshot.

Repositories opt in with `path_index_paths` in the config. During SlateDB-backed
backups, Vaultic writes path bindings in the same transaction as the metadata
revision that created, deleted, renamed, or rebound the path. Content-only and
metadata-only updates do not write path bindings, because the path still points
at the same inode. Hardlinked inodes write one binding per recorded path.

The new `vaultic index path-index` command builds or refreshes the index for an
existing repository, reports the number of snapshots scanned, bindings changed,
overflow paths, and estimated bytes written, and can prune bindings before a
commit sequence when older snapshots leave the retention window.

`vaultic index file-history` now uses the path index when entries are available
and confirms every indexed binding through the immutable snapshot walk before
reporting it. If the index is absent, it falls back to the Phase 13 walk.

